1
00:00:04,366 --> 00:00:09,565
WHEN they were gone, Elizabeth, as if intending to exasperate herself as much as possible against Mr.

2
00:00:09,565 --> 00:00:19,765
Darcy, chose for her employment the examination of all the letters which Jane had written to her since her being in Kent. They contained no actual complaint, nor was there any revival of

3
00:00:19,765 --> 00:00:25,166
past occurrences, or any communication of present suffering. But in all, and in almost every line

4
00:00:25,166 --> 00:00:29,433
of each, there was a want of that cheerfulness which had been used to characterize her style,

5
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:34,400
and which, proceeding from the serenity of a mind at ease with itself, and kindly disposed

6
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:40,166
towards everyone, had been scarcely ever clouded. Elizabeth noticed every sentence conveying the

7
00:00:40,166 --> 00:00:45,866
idea of uneasiness, with an attention which it had hardly received on the first perusal. Mr. Darcy’s

8
00:00:45,866 --> 00:00:50,832
shameful boast of what misery he had been able to inflict gave her a keener sense of her sister’s

9
00:00:50,866 --> 00:00:55,599
sufferings. It was some consolation to think that his visit to Rosings was to end on the day after

10
00:00:55,599 --> 00:01:01,099
the next, and a still greater that in less than a fortnight she should herself be with Jane again,

11
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:10,065
and enabled to contribute to the recovery of her spirits, by all that affection could do. She could not think of Darcy’s leaving Kent without remembering that his cousin was to go

12
00:01:10,065 --> 00:01:13,832
with him; but Colonel Fitzwilliam had made it clear that he had no intentions at all,

13
00:01:13,832 --> 00:01:18,032
and, agreeable as he was, she did not mean to be unhappy about him.

14
00:01:18,032 --> 00:01:21,765
While settling this point, she was suddenly roused by the sound of the door-bell;

15
00:01:21,765 --> 00:01:25,832
and her spirits were a little fluttered by the idea of its being Colonel Fitzwilliam himself,

16
00:01:25,832 --> 00:01:30,765
who had once before called late in the evening, and might now come to inquire particularly after

17
00:01:30,765 --> 00:01:35,133
her. But this idea was soon banished, and her spirits were very differently affected,

18
00:01:35,133 --> 00:01:40,400
when, to her utter amazement, she saw Mr. Darcy walk into the room. In a hurried

19
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:44,565
manner he immediately began an inquiry after her health, imputing his visit to a wish of hearing

20
00:01:44,565 --> 00:01:50,599
that she were better. She answered him with cold civility. He sat down for a few moments, and then

21
00:01:50,599 --> 00:01:56,599
getting up walked about the room. Elizabeth was surprised, but said not a word. After a

22
00:01:56,599 --> 00:02:01,400
silence of several minutes, he came towards her in an agitated manner, and thus began:—

23
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:06,933
“In vain have I struggled. It will not do. My feelings will not be repressed.

24
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:11,200
You must allow me to tell you how ardently I admire and love you.”

25
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:17,666
Elizabeth’s astonishment was beyond expression. She stared, coloured, doubted,

26
00:02:17,666 --> 00:02:23,133
and was silent. This he considered sufficient encouragement, and the avowal of all that he felt

27
00:02:23,133 --> 00:02:28,933
and had long felt for her immediately followed. He spoke well; but there were feelings besides

28
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:33,200
those of the heart to be detailed, and he was not more eloquent on the subject of tenderness

29
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:39,099
than of pride. His sense of her inferiority, of its being a degradation, of the family

30
00:02:39,099 --> 00:02:43,366
obstacles which judgment had always opposed to inclination, were dwelt on with a warmth which

31
00:02:43,366 --> 00:02:47,900
seemed due to the consequence he was wounding, but was very unlikely to recommend his suit.

32
00:02:48,565 --> 00:02:53,000
In spite of her deeply-rooted dislike, she could not be insensible to the compliment

33
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,900
of such a man’s affection, and though her intentions did not vary for an instant,

34
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:02,299
she was at first sorry for the pain he was to receive; till roused to resentment by his

35
00:03:02,299 --> 00:03:07,466
subsequent language, she lost all compassion in anger. She tried, however, to compose herself

36
00:03:07,466 --> 00:03:12,166
to answer him with patience, when he should have done. He concluded with representing to her the

37
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:17,065
strength of that attachment which in spite of all his endeavours he had found impossible to conquer;

38
00:03:17,065 --> 00:03:27,433
and with expressing his hope that it would now be rewarded by her acceptance of his hand. As he said this she could easily see that he had no doubt of a favourable answer. He spoke of

39
00:03:27,433 --> 00:03:32,832
apprehension and anxiety, but his countenance expressed real security. Such a circumstance

40
00:03:32,866 --> 00:03:37,832
could only exasperate farther; and when he ceased the colour rose into her cheeks and she said,—

41
00:03:38,433 --> 00:03:47,832
“In such cases as this, it is, I believe, the established mode to express a sense of obligation for the sentiments avowed, however unequally they may be returned. It is natural

42
00:03:47,866 --> 00:03:53,133
that obligation should be felt, and if I could feel gratitude, I would now thank you. But I

43
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:59,265
cannot—I have never desired your good opinion, and you have certainly bestowed it most unwillingly.

44
00:03:59,265 --> 00:04:04,599
I am sorry to have occasioned pain to anyone. It has been most unconsciously done, however, and I

45
00:04:04,599 --> 00:04:09,400
hope will be of short duration. The feelings which you tell me have long prevented the acknowledgment

46
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:13,666
of your regard can have little difficulty in overcoming it after this explanation.”

47
00:04:14,299 --> 00:04:24,265
Mr. Darcy, who was leaning against the mantel-piece with his eyes fixed on her face, seemed to catch her words with no less resentment than surprise. His complexion became pale with

48
00:04:24,265 --> 00:04:29,133
anger, and the disturbance of his mind was visible in every feature. He was struggling for the

49
00:04:29,133 --> 00:04:33,565
appearance of composure, and would not open his lips till he believed himself to have attained it.

50
00:04:33,565 --> 00:04:39,400
The pause was to Elizabeth’s feelings dreadful. At length, in a voice of forced calmness, he said,—

51
00:04:39,933 --> 00:04:49,166
“And this is all the reply which I am to have the honour of expecting! I might, perhaps, wish to be informed why, with so little endeavour at civility, I am thus

52
00:04:49,166 --> 00:04:55,366
rejected. But it is of small importance.” “I might as well inquire,” replied she,

53
00:04:55,366 --> 00:04:59,666
“why, with so evident a design of offending and insulting me, you chose to tell me that

54
00:04:59,666 --> 00:05:04,065
you liked me against your will, against your reason, and even against your character? Was

55
00:05:04,065 --> 00:05:09,765
not this some excuse for incivility, if I was uncivil? But I have other provocations. You know

56
00:05:09,765 --> 00:05:14,433
I have. Had not my own feelings decided against you, had they been indifferent,

57
00:05:14,433 --> 00:05:18,866
or had they even been favourable, do you think that any consideration would tempt me to accept

58
00:05:18,866 --> 00:05:23,733
the man who has been the means of ruining, perhaps for ever, the happiness of a most beloved sister?”

59
00:05:24,565 --> 00:05:29,299
As she pronounced these words, Mr. Darcy changed colour; but the emotion was short,

60
00:05:29,299 --> 00:05:37,832
and he listened without attempting to interrupt her while she continued,— “I have every reason in the world to think ill of you. No motive can excuse the unjust

61
00:05:37,832 --> 00:05:43,633
and ungenerous part you acted there. You dare not, you cannot deny that you have been the principal,

62
00:05:43,666 --> 00:05:48,099
if not the only means of dividing them from each other, of exposing one to the censure of the world

63
00:05:48,099 --> 00:05:53,133
for caprice and instability, the other to its derision for disappointed hopes, and involving

64
00:05:53,133 --> 00:05:59,200
them both in misery of the acutest kind.” She paused, and saw with no slight indignation

65
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:02,065
that he was listening with an air which proved him wholly unmoved by

66
00:06:02,065 --> 00:06:06,565
any feeling of remorse. He even looked at her with a smile of affected incredulity.

67
00:06:07,099 --> 00:06:09,799
“Can you deny that you have done it?” she repeated.

68
00:06:09,799 --> 00:06:20,133
With assumed tranquillity he then replied, “I have no wish of denying that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I rejoice in my success. Towards

69
00:06:20,133 --> 00:06:25,666
him I have been kinder than towards myself.” Elizabeth disdained the appearance of noticing

70
00:06:25,666 --> 00:06:30,400
this civil reflection, but its meaning did not escape, nor was it likely to conciliate her.

71
00:06:31,099 --> 00:06:42,866
“But it is not merely this affair,” she continued, “on which my dislike is founded. Long before it had taken place, my opinion of you was decided. Your character was unfolded in the recital which

72
00:06:42,866 --> 00:06:47,799
I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. On this subject, what can you have to say?

73
00:06:47,799 --> 00:06:53,733
In what imaginary act of friendship can you here defend yourself? or under what misrepresentation

74
00:06:53,733 --> 00:06:58,166
can you here impose upon others?” “You take an eager interest in that

75
00:06:58,166 --> 00:07:02,799
gentleman’s concerns,” said Darcy, in a less tranquil tone, and with a heightened colour.

76
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:07,599
“Who that knows what his misfortunes have been can help feeling an interest in him?”

77
00:07:07,599 --> 00:07:12,200
“His misfortunes!” repeated Darcy, contemptuously,—“yes,

78
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:18,099
his misfortunes have been great indeed.” “And of your infliction,” cried Elizabeth,

79
00:07:18,099 --> 00:07:24,265
with energy; “You have reduced him to his present state of poverty—comparative poverty. You have

80
00:07:24,265 --> 00:07:28,765
withheld the advantages which you must know to have been designed for him. You have deprived the

81
00:07:28,765 --> 00:07:33,700
best years of his life of that independence which was no less his due than his desert. You have done

82
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:39,099
all this! and yet you can treat the mention of his misfortunes with contempt and ridicule.”

83
00:07:39,866 --> 00:07:46,332
“And this,” cried Darcy, as he walked with quick steps across the room, “is your opinion of me!

84
00:07:46,366 --> 00:07:52,832
This is the estimation in which you hold me! I thank you for explaining it so fully. My faults,

85
00:07:52,832 --> 00:07:58,599
according to this calculation, are heavy indeed! But, perhaps,” added he, stopping in his walk, and

86
00:07:58,599 --> 00:08:04,000
turning towards her, “these offences might have been overlooked, had not your pride been hurt by

87
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,966
my honest confession of the scruples that had long prevented my forming any serious design. These

88
00:08:08,966 --> 00:08:13,832
bitter accusations might have been suppressed, had I, with greater policy, concealed my struggles,

89
00:08:13,832 --> 00:08:18,866
and flattered you into the belief of my being impelled by unqualified, unalloyed inclination;

90
00:08:18,866 --> 00:08:24,765
by reason, by reflection, by everything. But disguise of every sort is my abhorrence. Nor

91
00:08:24,765 --> 00:08:29,433
am I ashamed of the feelings I related. They were natural and just. Could you expect me to

92
00:08:29,433 --> 00:08:34,366
rejoice in the inferiority of your connections?—to congratulate myself on the hope of relations whose

93
00:08:34,366 --> 00:08:39,166
condition in life is so decidedly beneath my own?” Elizabeth felt herself growing more angry every

94
00:08:39,166 --> 00:08:43,099
moment; yet she tried to the utmost to speak with composure when she said,—

95
00:08:43,765 --> 00:08:47,299
“You are mistaken, Mr. Darcy, if you suppose that the mode of your

96
00:08:47,299 --> 00:08:51,633
declaration affected me in any other way than as it spared me the concern which I

97
00:08:51,633 --> 00:08:55,765
might have felt in refusing you, had you behaved in a more gentlemanlike manner.”

98
00:08:56,565 --> 00:09:01,000
She saw him start at this; but he said nothing, and she continued,—

99
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,166
“You could not have made me the offer of your hand in any possible way that

100
00:09:04,166 --> 00:09:08,366
would have tempted me to accept it.” Again his astonishment was obvious;

101
00:09:08,366 --> 00:09:13,233
and he looked at her with an expression of mingled incredulity and mortification. She went on,—

102
00:09:13,765 --> 00:09:23,265
“From the very beginning, from the first moment, I may almost say, of my acquaintance with you, your manners impressing me with the fullest belief of your arrogance, your conceit, and your selfish

103
00:09:23,265 --> 00:09:27,400
disdain of the feelings of others, were such as to form that groundwork of disapprobation,

104
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:37,099
on which succeeding events have built so immovable a dislike; and I had not known you a month before I felt that you were the last man in the world whom I could ever be prevailed on to marry.”

105
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,599
“You have said quite enough, madam. I perfectly comprehend your feelings,

106
00:09:41,599 --> 00:09:50,200
and have now only to be ashamed of what my own have been. Forgive me for having taken up so much of your time, and accept my best wishes for your health and happiness.”

107
00:09:51,332 --> 00:09:56,265
And with these words he hastily left the room, and Elizabeth heard him the next moment open the

108
00:09:56,265 --> 00:10:02,065
front door and quit the house. The tumult of her mind was now painfully great. She knew not how

109
00:10:02,065 --> 00:10:12,866
to support herself, and, from actual weakness, sat down and cried for half an hour. Her astonishment, as she reflected on what had passed, was increased by every review of it. That she should receive an

110
00:10:12,866 --> 00:10:17,299
offer of marriage from Mr. Darcy! that he should have been in love with her for so many months!

111
00:10:17,299 --> 00:10:21,700
so much in love as to wish to marry her in spite of all the objections which had made him prevent

112
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:26,000
his friend’s marrying her sister, and which must appear at least with equal force in his own case,

113
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,166
was almost incredible! it was gratifying to have inspired unconsciously so strong an affection.

114
00:10:31,166 --> 00:10:37,599
But his pride, his abominable pride, his shameless avowal of what he had done with respect to Jane,

115
00:10:37,599 --> 00:10:42,765
his unpardonable assurance in acknowledging, though he could not justify it, and the unfeeling

116
00:10:42,765 --> 00:10:47,000
manner which he had mentioned Mr. Wickham, his cruelty towards whom he had not attempted to deny,

117
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,433
soon overcame the pity which the consideration of his attachment had for a moment excited.

118
00:10:51,433 --> 00:10:56,299
She continued in very agitating reflections till the sound of Lady Catherine’s carriage made her

119
00:10:56,299 --> 00:11:02,966
feel how unequal she was to encounter Charlotte’s observation, and hurried her away to her room.

120
00:11:02,966 --> 00:11:03,933
CHAPTER XXXV.

121
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:16,765
ELIZABETH awoke the next morning to the same thoughts and meditations which had at length

122
00:11:16,765 --> 00:11:22,233
closed her eyes. She could not yet recover from the surprise of what had happened: it was

123
00:11:22,233 --> 00:11:28,466
impossible to think of anything else; and, totally indisposed for employment, she resolved soon after

124
00:11:28,466 --> 00:11:35,065
breakfast to indulge herself in air and exercise. She was proceeding directly to her favourite walk,

125
00:11:35,065 --> 00:11:40,466
when the recollection of Mr. Darcy’s sometimes coming there stopped her, and instead of entering

126
00:11:40,466 --> 00:11:50,799
the park, she turned up the lane which led her farther from the turnpike road. The park paling was still the boundary on one side, and she soon passed one of the gates into the ground.

127
00:11:51,765 --> 00:11:56,666
After walking two or three times along that part of the lane, she was tempted, by the pleasantness

128
00:11:56,666 --> 00:12:01,532
of the morning, to stop at the gates and look into the park. The five weeks which she had now

129
00:12:01,532 --> 00:12:06,200
passed in Kent had made a great difference in the country, and every day was adding to

130
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:11,799
the verdure of the early trees. She was on the point of continuing her walk, when she caught a

131
00:12:11,799 --> 00:12:16,599
glimpse of a gentleman within the sort of grove which edged the park: he was moving that way;

132
00:12:16,599 --> 00:12:22,299
and fearful of its being Mr. Darcy, she was directly retreating. But the person who advanced

133
00:12:22,299 --> 00:12:27,565
was now near enough to see her, and stepping forward with eagerness, pronounced her name. She

134
00:12:27,565 --> 00:12:38,799
had turned away; but on hearing herself called, though in a voice which proved it to be Mr. Darcy, she moved again towards the gate. He had by that time reached it also; and, holding out a letter,

135
00:12:38,799 --> 00:12:44,200
which she instinctively took, said, with a look of haughty composure, “I have been walking in

136
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:48,400
the grove some time, in the hope of meeting you. Will you do me the honour of reading that letter?”

137
00:12:49,233 --> 00:13:00,700
and then, with a slight bow, turned again into the plantation, and was soon out of sight. With no expectation of pleasure, but with the strongest curiosity, Elizabeth opened the letter,

138
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:11,733
and to her still increasing wonder, perceived an envelope containing two sheets of letter paper, written quite through, in a very close hand. The envelope itself was likewise full.

139
00:13:12,332 --> 00:13:17,666
Pursuing her way along the lane, she then began it. It was dated from Rosings, at

140
00:13:17,666 --> 00:13:23,866
eight o’clock in the morning, and was as follows:— “Be not alarmed, madam, on receiving this letter,

141
00:13:23,866 --> 00:13:35,000
by the apprehension of its containing any repetition of those sentiments, or renewal of those offers, which were last night so disgusting to you. I write without any intention of paining

142
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:41,133
you, or humbling myself, by dwelling on wishes, which, for the happiness of both, cannot be too

143
00:13:41,133 --> 00:13:46,099
soon forgotten; and the effort which the formation and the perusal of this letter must occasion,

144
00:13:46,099 --> 00:13:52,599
should have been spared, had not my character required it to be written and read. You must,

145
00:13:52,599 --> 00:13:57,799
therefore, pardon the freedom with which I demand your attention; your feelings, I know, will bestow

146
00:13:57,799 --> 00:14:04,466
it unwillingly, but I demand it of your justice. “Two offences of a very different nature,

147
00:14:04,466 --> 00:14:11,433
and by no means of equal magnitude, you last night laid to my charge. The first mentioned was,

148
00:14:11,433 --> 00:14:16,966
that, regardless of the sentiments of either, I had detached Mr. Bingley from your sister,—and

149
00:14:17,565 --> 00:14:22,933
the other, that I had, in defiance of various claims, in defiance of honour and humanity,

150
00:14:22,933 --> 00:14:29,966
ruined the immediate prosperity and blasted the prospects of Mr. Wickham. Wilfully and wantonly

151
00:14:29,966 --> 00:14:35,133
to have thrown off the companion of my youth, the acknowledged favourite of my father, a young man

152
00:14:35,133 --> 00:14:39,866
who had scarcely any other dependence than on our patronage, and who had been brought up to expect

153
00:14:39,866 --> 00:14:46,166
its exertion, would be a depravity, to which the separation of two young persons whose affection

154
00:14:46,166 --> 00:14:52,166
could be the growth of only a few weeks, could bear no comparison. But from the severity of that

155
00:14:52,166 --> 00:14:58,000
blame which was last night so liberally bestowed, respecting each circumstance, I shall hope to be

156
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:02,733
in future secured, when the following account of my actions and their motives has been read.

157
00:15:03,265 --> 00:15:08,433
If, in the explanation of them which is due to myself, I am under the necessity of relating

158
00:15:08,433 --> 00:15:13,832
feelings which may be offensive to yours, I can only say that I am sorry. The necessity must be

159
00:15:13,832 --> 00:15:20,065
obeyed, and further apology would be absurd. I had not been long in Hertfordshire before I saw,

160
00:15:20,065 --> 00:15:24,500
in common with others, that Bingley preferred your elder sister to any other young woman in the

161
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:29,500
country. But it was not till the evening of the dance at Netherfield that I had any apprehension

162
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:35,900
of his feeling a serious attachment. I had often seen him in love before. At that ball, while I

163
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:41,265
had the honour of dancing with you, I was first made acquainted, by Sir William Lucas’s accidental

164
00:15:41,265 --> 00:15:46,065
information, that Bingley’s attentions to your sister had given rise to a general expectation

165
00:15:46,065 --> 00:15:51,933
of their marriage. He spoke of it as a certain event, of which the time alone could be undecided.

166
00:15:51,933 --> 00:15:56,900
From that moment I observed my friend’s behaviour attentively; and I could then

167
00:15:56,900 --> 00:16:01,332
perceive that his partiality for Miss Bennet was beyond what I had ever witnessed in him.

168
00:16:01,366 --> 00:16:07,666
Your sister I also watched. Her look and manners were open, cheerful, and engaging as ever,

169
00:16:07,666 --> 00:16:13,366
but without any symptom of peculiar regard; and I remained convinced, from the evening’s scrutiny,

170
00:16:13,366 --> 00:16:17,200
that though she received his attentions with pleasure, she did not invite them

171
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:22,666
by any participation of sentiment. If you have not been mistaken here, I must have been in an

172
00:16:22,666 --> 00:16:28,599
error. Your superior knowledge of your sister must make the latter probable. If it be so, if

173
00:16:28,599 --> 00:16:33,933
I have been misled by such error to inflict pain on her, your resentment has not been unreasonable.

174
00:16:34,466 --> 00:16:39,332
But I shall not scruple to assert, that the serenity of your sister’s countenance and air was

175
00:16:39,332 --> 00:16:44,065
such as might have given the most acute observer a conviction that, however amiable her temper,

176
00:16:44,065 --> 00:16:49,666
her heart was not likely to be easily touched. That I was desirous of believing her indifferent

177
00:16:49,666 --> 00:16:54,099
is certain; but I will venture to say that my investigations and decisions are not

178
00:16:54,099 --> 00:16:59,866
usually influenced by my hopes or fears. I did not believe her to be indifferent because I wished it;

179
00:16:59,866 --> 00:17:06,500
I believed it on impartial conviction, as truly as I wished it in reason. My objections to the

180
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:10,466
marriage were not merely those which I last night acknowledged to have required the utmost force of

181
00:17:10,466 --> 00:17:16,166
passion to put aside in my own case; the want of connection could not be so great an evil to my

182
00:17:16,166 --> 00:17:22,065
friend as to me. But there were other causes of repugnance; causes which, though still existing,

183
00:17:22,065 --> 00:17:27,866
and existing to an equal degree in both instances, I had myself endeavoured to forget, because they

184
00:17:27,866 --> 00:17:34,299
were not immediately before me. These causes must be stated, though briefly. The situation of your

185
00:17:34,299 --> 00:17:39,265
mother’s family, though objectionable, was nothing in comparison of that total want of propriety so

186
00:17:39,265 --> 00:17:51,866
frequently, so almost uniformly betrayed by herself, by your three younger sisters, and occasionally even by your father:—pardon me,—it pains me to offend you. But amidst your concern

187
00:17:51,866 --> 00:17:56,599
for the defects of your nearest relations, and your displeasure at this representation of them,

188
00:17:56,599 --> 00:18:07,633
let it give you consolation to consider that to have conducted yourselves so as to avoid any share of the like censure is praise no less generally bestowed on you and your eldest sister than it

189
00:18:07,666 --> 00:18:13,065
is honourable to the sense and disposition of both. I will only say, farther, that from what

190
00:18:13,065 --> 00:18:19,099
passed that evening my opinion of all parties was confirmed, and every inducement heightened, which

191
00:18:19,099 --> 00:18:23,966
could have led me before to preserve my friend from what I esteemed a most unhappy connection.

192
00:18:24,765 --> 00:18:29,000
He left Netherfield for London on the day following, as you, I am certain, remember,

193
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:36,200
with the design of soon returning. The part which I acted is now to be explained. His sisters’

194
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:41,500
uneasiness had been equally excited with my own: our coincidence of feeling was soon discovered;

195
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:47,000
and, alike sensible that no time was to be lost in detaching their brother, we shortly resolved

196
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:52,700
on joining him directly in London. We accordingly went—and there I readily engaged in the office of

197
00:18:52,700 --> 00:18:57,765
pointing out to my friend the certain evils of such a choice. I described and enforced them

198
00:18:57,765 --> 00:19:02,500
earnestly. But however this remonstrance might have staggered or delayed his determination,

199
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:07,332
I do not suppose that it would ultimately have prevented the marriage, had it not been seconded

200
00:19:07,366 --> 00:19:18,765
by the assurance, which I hesitated not in giving, of your sister’s indifference. He had before believed her to return his affection with sincere, if not with equal, regard. But Bingley has great

201
00:19:18,765 --> 00:19:23,866
natural modesty, with a stronger dependence on my judgment than on his own. To convince him,

202
00:19:23,866 --> 00:19:28,299
therefore, that he had deceived himself was no very difficult point. To persuade him against

203
00:19:28,299 --> 00:19:33,565
returning into Hertfordshire, when that conviction had been given, was scarcely the work of a moment.

204
00:19:33,565 --> 00:19:44,366
I cannot blame myself for having done thus much. There is but one part of my conduct, in the whole affair, on which I do not reflect with satisfaction; it is that I condescended to adopt

205
00:19:44,366 --> 00:19:49,466
the measures of art so far as to conceal from him your sister’s being in town. I knew it myself,

206
00:19:49,466 --> 00:19:54,866
as it was known to Miss Bingley; but her brother is even yet ignorant of it. That they might have

207
00:19:54,866 --> 00:19:59,565
met without ill consequence is, perhaps, probable; but his regard did not appear to me enough

208
00:19:59,565 --> 00:20:05,565
extinguished for him to see her without some danger. Perhaps this concealment, this disguise,

209
00:20:05,565 --> 00:20:11,000
was beneath me. It is done, however, and it was done for the best. On this subject I have nothing

210
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:17,133
more to say, no other apology to offer. If I have wounded your sister’s feelings, it was unknowingly

211
00:20:17,133 --> 00:20:21,765
done; and though the motives which governed me may to you very naturally appear insufficient,

212
00:20:21,765 --> 00:20:28,466
I have not yet learnt to condemn them.—With respect to that other, more weighty accusation,

213
00:20:28,466 --> 00:20:32,666
of having injured Mr. Wickham, I can only refute it by laying before you the whole of

214
00:20:32,666 --> 00:20:38,666
his connection with my family. Of what he has particularly accused me I am ignorant; but of

215
00:20:38,666 --> 00:20:43,599
the truth of what I shall relate I can summon more than one witness of undoubted veracity.

216
00:20:44,666 --> 00:20:49,299
Mr. Wickham is the son of a very respectable man, who had for many years the management of

217
00:20:49,299 --> 00:20:54,133
all the Pemberley estates, and whose good conduct in the discharge of his trust naturally inclined

218
00:20:54,166 --> 00:20:59,866
my father to be of service to him; and on George Wickham, who was his godson, his kindness was

219
00:20:59,866 --> 00:21:05,265
therefore liberally bestowed. My father supported him at school, and afterwards at Cambridge;

220
00:21:05,265 --> 00:21:11,299
most important assistance, as his own father, always poor from the extravagance of his wife,

221
00:21:11,299 --> 00:21:22,366
would have been unable to give him a gentleman’s education. My father was not only fond of this young man’s society, whose manners were always engaging, he had also the highest opinion of him,

222
00:21:22,366 --> 00:21:28,299
and hoping the church would be his profession, intended to provide for him in it. As for myself,

223
00:21:28,299 --> 00:21:33,633
it is many, many years since I first began to think of him in a very different manner. The

224
00:21:33,633 --> 00:21:38,433
vicious propensities, the want of principle, which he was careful to guard from the knowledge of his

225
00:21:38,433 --> 00:21:43,466
best friend, could not escape the observation of a young man of nearly the same age with himself, and

226
00:21:43,466 --> 00:21:48,866
who had opportunities of seeing him in unguarded moments, which Mr. Darcy could not have. Here

227
00:21:48,866 --> 00:21:54,633
again I shall give you pain—to what degree you only can tell. But whatever may be the sentiments

228
00:21:54,633 --> 00:21:59,265
which Mr. Wickham has created, a suspicion of their nature shall not prevent me from unfolding

229
00:21:59,265 --> 00:22:06,032
his real character. It adds even another motive. My excellent father died about five years ago;

230
00:22:06,032 --> 00:22:11,200
and his attachment to Mr. Wickham was to the last so steady, that in his will he particularly

231
00:22:11,200 --> 00:22:15,565
recommended it to me to promote his advancement in the best manner that his profession might allow,

232
00:22:15,565 --> 00:22:27,433
and if he took orders, desired that a valuable family living might be his as soon as it became vacant. There was also a legacy of one thousand pounds. His own father did not long survive mine;

233
00:22:27,433 --> 00:22:32,799
and within half a year from these events Mr. Wickham wrote to inform me that, having finally

234
00:22:32,799 --> 00:22:37,933
resolved against taking orders, he hoped I should not think it unreasonable for him to expect some

235
00:22:37,933 --> 00:22:44,133
more immediate pecuniary advantage, in lieu of the preferment, by which he could not be benefited. He

236
00:22:44,133 --> 00:22:48,433
had some intention, he added, of studying the law, and I must be aware that the interest of

237
00:22:48,433 --> 00:22:53,799
one thousand pounds would be a very insufficient support therein. I rather wished than believed

238
00:22:53,799 --> 00:22:59,500
him to be sincere; but, at any rate, was perfectly ready to accede to his proposal. I knew that Mr.

239
00:22:59,500 --> 00:23:04,700
Wickham ought not to be a clergyman. The business was therefore soon settled. He resigned all claim

240
00:23:04,700 --> 00:23:08,733
to assistance in the church, were it possible that he could ever be in a situation to receive it,

241
00:23:08,733 --> 00:23:19,700
and accepted in return three thousand pounds. All connection between us seemed now dissolved. I thought too ill of him to invite him to Pemberley, or admit his society in town.

242
00:23:20,299 --> 00:23:24,799
In town, I believe, he chiefly lived, but his studying the law was a mere pretence;

243
00:23:24,799 --> 00:23:31,233
and being now free from all restraint, his life was a life of idleness and dissipation. For about

244
00:23:31,233 --> 00:23:40,700
three years I heard little of him; but on the decease of the incumbent of the living which had been designed for him, he applied to me again by letter for the presentation. His circumstances,

245
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:45,799
he assured me, and I had no difficulty in believing it, were exceedingly bad. He had

246
00:23:45,799 --> 00:23:51,265
found the law a most unprofitable study, and was now absolutely resolved on being ordained, if I

247
00:23:51,265 --> 00:23:55,065
would present him to the living in question—of which he trusted there could be little doubt,

248
00:23:55,065 --> 00:23:59,500
as he was well assured that I had no other person to provide for, and I could not have forgotten my

249
00:23:59,500 --> 00:24:04,933
revered father’s intentions. You will hardly blame me for refusing to comply with this entreaty,

250
00:24:04,933 --> 00:24:10,233
or for resisting every repetition of it. His resentment was in proportion to the distress of

251
00:24:10,233 --> 00:24:15,265
his circumstances—and he was doubtless as violent in his abuse of me to others as in his reproaches

252
00:24:15,265 --> 00:24:20,966
to myself. After this period, every appearance of acquaintance was dropped. How he lived,

253
00:24:20,966 --> 00:24:26,065
I know not. But last summer he was again most painfully obtruded on my notice.

254
00:24:26,599 --> 00:24:31,900
I must now mention a circumstance which I would wish to forget myself, and which no obligation

255
00:24:31,900 --> 00:24:36,765
less than the present should induce me to unfold to any human being. Having said thus much,

256
00:24:36,765 --> 00:24:43,099
I feel no doubt of your secrecy. My sister, who is more than ten years my junior, was left to the

257
00:24:43,099 --> 00:24:48,166
guardianship of my mother’s nephew, Colonel Fitzwilliam, and myself. About a year ago,

258
00:24:48,166 --> 00:24:59,433
she was taken from school, and an establishment formed for her in London; and last summer she went with the lady who presided over it to Ramsgate; and thither also went Mr. Wickham, undoubtedly

259
00:24:59,433 --> 00:25:10,765
by design; for there proved to have been a prior acquaintance between him and Mrs. Younge, in whose character we were most unhappily deceived; and by her connivance and aid he so far recommended

260
00:25:10,765 --> 00:25:15,166
himself to Georgiana, whose affectionate heart retained a strong impression of his kindness

261
00:25:15,166 --> 00:25:26,366
to her as a child, that she was persuaded to believe herself in love and to consent to an elopement. She was then but fifteen, which must be her excuse; and after stating her imprudence,

262
00:25:26,366 --> 00:25:31,933
I am happy to add, that I owed the knowledge of it to herself. I joined them unexpectedly a day

263
00:25:31,933 --> 00:25:37,299
or two before the intended elopement; and then Georgiana, unable to support the idea of grieving

264
00:25:37,299 --> 00:25:41,966
and offending a brother whom she almost looked up to as a father, acknowledged the whole to me.

265
00:25:41,966 --> 00:25:53,366
You may imagine what I felt and how I acted. Regard for my sister’s credit and feelings prevented any public exposure; but I wrote to Mr. Wickham, who left the place immediately, and Mrs.

266
00:25:53,366 --> 00:25:58,765
Younge was of course removed from her charge. Mr. Wickham’s chief object was unquestionably my

267
00:25:58,765 --> 00:26:03,565
sister’s fortune, which is thirty thousand pounds; but I cannot help supposing that the hope of

268
00:26:03,565 --> 00:26:10,366
revenging himself on me was a strong inducement. His revenge would have been complete indeed. This,

269
00:26:10,366 --> 00:26:14,966
madam, is a faithful narrative of every event in which we have been concerned together; and if you

270
00:26:14,966 --> 00:26:20,133
do not absolutely reject it as false, you will, I hope, acquit me henceforth of cruelty towards

271
00:26:20,133 --> 00:26:26,065
Mr. Wickham. I know not in what manner, under what form of falsehood, he has imposed on you; but his

272
00:26:26,065 --> 00:26:36,099
success is not perhaps to be wondered at, ignorant as you previously were of everything concerning either. Detection could not be in your power, and suspicion certainly not in your inclination.

273
00:26:37,099 --> 00:26:42,166
You may possibly wonder why all this was not told you last night. But I was not then master

274
00:26:42,166 --> 00:26:47,065
enough of myself to know what could or ought to be revealed. For the truth of everything

275
00:26:47,065 --> 00:26:52,065
here related, I can appeal more particularly to the testimony of Colonel Fitzwilliam, who,

276
00:26:52,065 --> 00:26:56,799
from our near relationship and constant intimacy, and still more as one of the executors of my

277
00:26:56,799 --> 00:27:01,933
father’s will, has been unavoidably acquainted with every particular of these transactions. If

278
00:27:01,933 --> 00:27:06,799
your abhorrence of me should make my assertions valueless, you cannot be prevented by the same

279
00:27:06,799 --> 00:27:12,400
cause from confiding in my cousin; and that there may be the possibility of consulting him, I shall

280
00:27:12,400 --> 00:27:16,099
endeavour to find some opportunity of putting this letter in your hands in the course of the morning.

281
00:27:16,765 --> 00:27:21,633
I will only add, God bless you. “Fitzwilliam Darcy.”

282
00:27:23,900 --> 00:27:33,133
CHAPTER XXXVI.

283
00:27:33,166 --> 00:27:38,065
ELIZABETH, when Mr. Darcy gave her the letter, did not expect it to contain a renewal of his offers,

284
00:27:38,065 --> 00:27:47,900
she had formed no expectation at all of its contents. But such as they were, it may be well supposed how eagerly she went through them, and what a contrariety of emotion they

285
00:27:47,900 --> 00:27:53,433
excited. Her feelings as she read were scarcely to be defined. With amazement did she first

286
00:27:53,433 --> 00:27:58,366
understand that he believed any apology to be in his power; and steadfastly was she persuaded,

287
00:27:58,366 --> 00:28:04,299
that he could have no explanation to give, which a just sense of shame would not conceal. With

288
00:28:04,299 --> 00:28:08,866
a strong prejudice against everything he might say, she began his account of what had happened

289
00:28:08,866 --> 00:28:18,400
at Netherfield. She read with an eagerness which hardly left her power of comprehension; and from impatience of knowing what the next sentence might bring, was incapable of attending

290
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:23,599
to the sense of the one before her eyes. His belief of her sister’s insensibility she instantly

291
00:28:23,599 --> 00:28:29,065
resolved to be false; and his account of the real, the worst objections to the match, made her too

292
00:28:29,065 --> 00:28:34,099
angry to have any wish of doing him justice. He expressed no regret for what he had done which

293
00:28:34,099 --> 00:28:40,000
satisfied her; his style was not penitent, but haughty. It was all pride and insolence.

294
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:45,799
But when this subject was succeeded by his account of Mr. Wickham—when she read, with somewhat

295
00:28:45,799 --> 00:28:50,733
clearer attention, a relation of events which, if true, must overthrow every cherished opinion of

296
00:28:50,733 --> 00:28:55,900
his worth, and which bore so alarming an affinity to his own history of himself—her feelings were

297
00:28:55,900 --> 00:29:01,099
yet more acutely painful and more difficult of definition. Astonishment, apprehension,

298
00:29:01,099 --> 00:29:07,065
and even horror, oppressed her. She wished to discredit it entirely, repeatedly exclaiming,

299
00:29:07,065 --> 00:29:13,933
“This must be false! This cannot be! This must be the grossest falsehood!”—and when she had gone

300
00:29:13,933 --> 00:29:17,200
through the whole letter, though scarcely knowing anything of the last page or two,

301
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:22,099
put it hastily away, protesting that she would not regard it, that she would never look in it again.

302
00:29:22,099 --> 00:29:33,400
In this perturbed state of mind, with thoughts that could rest on nothing, she walked on; but it would not do: in half a minute the letter was unfolded again; and collecting herself as well as

303
00:29:33,400 --> 00:29:38,599
she could, she again began the mortifying perusal of all that related to Wickham, and commanded

304
00:29:38,599 --> 00:29:48,332
herself so far as to examine the meaning of every sentence. The account of his connection with the Pemberley family was exactly what he had related himself; and the kindness of the late Mr. Darcy,

305
00:29:48,332 --> 00:29:52,466
though she had not before known its extent, agreed equally well with his own words.

306
00:29:53,065 --> 00:30:03,500
So far each recital confirmed the other; but when she came to the will, the difference was great. What Wickham had said of the living was fresh in her memory; and as she recalled his very words,

307
00:30:03,500 --> 00:30:08,433
it was impossible not to feel that there was gross duplicity on one side or the other, and,

308
00:30:08,433 --> 00:30:13,466
for a few moments, she flattered herself that her wishes did not err. But when she read and re-read,

309
00:30:13,466 --> 00:30:17,666
with the closest attention, the particulars immediately following of Wickham’s resigning

310
00:30:17,666 --> 00:30:21,166
all pretensions to the living, of his receiving in lieu so considerable a sum

311
00:30:21,166 --> 00:30:26,133
as three thousand pounds, again was she forced to hesitate. She put down the letter,

312
00:30:26,133 --> 00:30:31,765
weighed every circumstance with what she meant to be impartiality—deliberated on the probability of

313
00:30:31,765 --> 00:30:37,900
each statement—but with little success. On both sides it was only assertion. Again she read

314
00:30:37,900 --> 00:30:43,166
on. But every line proved more clearly that the affair, which she had believed it impossible that

315
00:30:43,166 --> 00:30:47,500
any contrivance could so represent as to render Mr. Darcy’s conduct in it less than infamous,

316
00:30:47,500 --> 00:30:51,700
was capable of a turn which must make him entirely blameless throughout the whole.

317
00:30:52,532 --> 00:30:57,265
The extravagance and general profligacy which he scrupled not to lay to Mr. Wickham’s charge

318
00:30:57,265 --> 00:31:02,666
exceedingly shocked her; the more so, as she could bring no proof of its injustice. She had

319
00:31:02,666 --> 00:31:06,933
never heard of him before his entrance into the ——shire militia, in which he had engaged at the

320
00:31:06,933 --> 00:31:11,733
persuasion of the young man, who, on meeting him accidentally in town, had there renewed a slight

321
00:31:11,733 --> 00:31:15,832
acquaintance. Of his former way of life, nothing had been known in Hertfordshire but what he told

322
00:31:15,832 --> 00:31:18,099
himself. As to his real character,

323
00:31:18,099 --> 00:31:23,599
had information been in her power, she had never felt a wish of inquiring. His countenance, voice,

324
00:31:23,599 --> 00:31:28,065
and manner, had established him at once in the possession of every virtue. She tried

325
00:31:28,065 --> 00:31:33,099
to recollect some instance of goodness, some distinguished trait of integrity or benevolence,

326
00:31:33,099 --> 00:31:38,265
that might rescue him from the attacks of Mr. Darcy; or at least, by the predominance of virtue,

327
00:31:38,265 --> 00:31:42,633
atone for those casual errors, under which she would endeavour to class what Mr. Darcy

328
00:31:42,633 --> 00:31:48,166
had described as the idleness and vice of many years’ continuance. But no such recollection

329
00:31:48,166 --> 00:31:58,466
befriended her. She could see him instantly before her, in every charm of air and address, but she could remember no more substantial good than the general approbation of the neighbourhood,

330
00:31:58,466 --> 00:32:03,866
and the regard which his social powers had gained him in the mess. After pausing on this point a

331
00:32:03,866 --> 00:32:10,065
considerable while, she once more continued to read. But, alas! the story which followed, of his

332
00:32:10,065 --> 00:32:14,966
designs on Miss Darcy, received some confirmation from what had passed between Colonel Fitzwilliam

333
00:32:14,966 --> 00:32:19,666
and herself only the morning before; and at last she was referred for the truth of every particular

334
00:32:19,666 --> 00:32:29,299
to Colonel Fitzwilliam himself—from whom she had previously received the information of his near concern in all his cousin’s affairs and whose character she had no reason to question. At

335
00:32:29,299 --> 00:32:34,166
one time she had almost resolved on applying to him, but the idea was checked by the awkwardness

336
00:32:34,166 --> 00:32:38,900
of the application, and at length wholly banished by the conviction that Mr. Darcy would never have

337
00:32:38,900 --> 00:32:43,099
hazarded such a proposal, if he had not been well assured of his cousin’s corroboration.

338
00:32:43,765 --> 00:32:47,565
She perfectly remembered everything that had passed in conversation between Wickham

339
00:32:47,565 --> 00:32:52,065
and herself in their first evening at Mr. Philips’s. Many of his expressions were still

340
00:32:52,065 --> 00:32:57,166
fresh in her memory. She was now struck with the impropriety of such communications to a stranger,

341
00:32:57,166 --> 00:33:02,000
and wondered it had escaped her before. She saw the indelicacy of putting himself forward

342
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:06,799
as he had done, and the inconsistency of his professions with his conduct. She remembered

343
00:33:06,799 --> 00:33:11,133
that he had boasted of having no fear of seeing Mr. Darcy—that Mr. Darcy might leave the country,

344
00:33:11,133 --> 00:33:15,565
but that he should stand his ground; yet he had avoided the Netherfield ball the

345
00:33:15,565 --> 00:33:25,900
very next week. She remembered, also, that till the Netherfield family had quitted the country, he had told his story to no one but herself; but that after their removal, it had been everywhere

346
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:30,866
discussed; that he had then no reserves, no scruples in sinking Mr. Darcy’s character,

347
00:33:30,866 --> 00:33:35,366
though he had assured her that respect for the father would always prevent his exposing the son.

348
00:33:35,366 --> 00:33:41,133
How differently did everything now appear in which he was concerned! His attentions to Miss King were

349
00:33:41,133 --> 00:33:46,166
now the consequence of views solely and hatefully mercenary; and the mediocrity of her fortune

350
00:33:46,166 --> 00:33:51,866
proved no longer the moderation of his wishes, but his eagerness to grasp at anything. His behaviour

351
00:33:51,866 --> 00:33:57,032
to herself could now have had no tolerable motive: he had either been deceived with regard to her

352
00:33:57,032 --> 00:34:01,900
fortune, or had been gratifying his vanity by encouraging the preference which she believed

353
00:34:01,900 --> 00:34:07,299
she had most incautiously shown. Every lingering struggle in his favour grew fainter and fainter;

354
00:34:07,299 --> 00:34:11,900
and in further justification of Mr. Darcy, she could not but allow that Mr. Bingley,

355
00:34:11,900 --> 00:34:16,933
when questioned by Jane, had long ago asserted his blamelessness in the affair;—that,

356
00:34:16,933 --> 00:34:20,900
proud and repulsive as were his manners, she had never, in the whole course of their

357
00:34:20,900 --> 00:34:25,332
acquaintance—an acquaintance which had latterly brought them much together, and given her a sort

358
00:34:25,366 --> 00:34:29,666
of intimacy with his ways—seen anything that betrayed him to be unprincipled or

359
00:34:29,666 --> 00:34:35,366
unjust—anything that spoke him of irreligious or immoral habits;—that among his own connections

360
00:34:35,366 --> 00:34:40,400
he was esteemed and valued;—that even Wickham had allowed him merit as a brother, and that

361
00:34:40,400 --> 00:34:45,133
she had often heard him speak so affectionately of his sister as to prove him capable of some

362
00:34:45,166 --> 00:34:55,000
amiable feeling;—that had his actions been what Wickham represented them, so gross a violation of everything right could hardly have been concealed from the world; and that friendship between a

363
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:59,200
person capable of it and such an amiable man as Mr. Bingley was incomprehensible.

364
00:34:59,966 --> 00:35:05,799
She grew absolutely ashamed of herself. Of neither Darcy nor Wickham could she think,

365
00:35:05,799 --> 00:35:10,099
without feeling that she had been blind, partial, prejudiced, absurd.

366
00:35:10,933 --> 00:35:17,733
“How despicably have I acted!” she cried. “I, who have prided myself on my discernment! I,

367
00:35:17,733 --> 00:35:29,866
who have valued myself on my abilities! who have often disdained the generous candour of my sister, and gratified my vanity in useless or blameless distrust. How humiliating is this discovery! Yet,

368
00:35:29,866 --> 00:35:35,565
how just a humiliation! Had I been in love, I could not have been more wretchedly blind. But

369
00:35:35,565 --> 00:35:39,633
vanity, not love, has been my folly. Pleased with the preference of one,

370
00:35:39,633 --> 00:35:43,065
and offended by the neglect of the other, on the very beginning of our acquaintance,

371
00:35:43,065 --> 00:35:48,099
I have courted prepossession and ignorance, and driven reason away where either were

372
00:35:48,099 --> 00:35:57,032
concerned. Till this moment, I never knew myself.” From herself to Jane, from Jane to Bingley, her

373
00:35:57,032 --> 00:36:01,400
thoughts were in a line which soon brought to her recollection that Mr. Darcy’s explanation there

374
00:36:01,400 --> 00:36:07,299
had appeared very insufficient; and she read it again. Widely different was the effect of a second

375
00:36:07,299 --> 00:36:12,633
perusal. How could she deny that credit to his assertions, in one instance, which she had been

376
00:36:12,633 --> 00:36:17,666
obliged to give in the other? He declared himself to have been totally unsuspicious of her sister’s

377
00:36:17,666 --> 00:36:22,466
attachment; and she could not help remembering what Charlotte’s opinion had always been. Neither

378
00:36:22,466 --> 00:36:27,099
could she deny the justice of his description of Jane. She felt that Jane’s feelings, though

379
00:36:27,099 --> 00:36:32,065
fervent, were little displayed, and that there was a constant complacency in her air and manner,

380
00:36:32,065 --> 00:36:37,065
not often united with great sensibility. When she came to that part of the letter in

381
00:36:37,065 --> 00:36:42,332
which her family were mentioned, in tones of such mortifying, yet merited, reproach, her sense of

382
00:36:42,332 --> 00:36:48,133
shame was severe. The justice of the charge struck her too forcibly for denial; and the circumstances

383
00:36:48,166 --> 00:36:58,633
to which he particularly alluded, as having passed at the Netherfield ball, and as confirming all his first disapprobation, could not have made a stronger impression on his mind than on hers.

384
00:36:58,666 --> 00:37:02,966
The compliment to herself and her sister was not unfelt. It soothed, but it could

385
00:37:02,966 --> 00:37:07,466
not console her for the contempt which had been thus self-attracted by the rest of her family;

386
00:37:07,466 --> 00:37:11,599
and as she considered that Jane’s disappointment had, in fact, been the work of her nearest

387
00:37:11,599 --> 00:37:15,933
relations, and reflected how materially the credit of both must be hurt by such

388
00:37:15,933 --> 00:37:20,733
impropriety of conduct, she felt depressed beyond anything she had ever known before.

389
00:37:21,500 --> 00:37:26,000
After wandering along the lane for two hours, giving way to every variety of thought,

390
00:37:26,599 --> 00:37:31,099
reconsidering events, determining probabilities, and reconciling herself, as well as she could,

391
00:37:31,099 --> 00:37:37,233
to a change so sudden and so important, fatigue, and a recollection of her long absence, made her

392
00:37:37,233 --> 00:37:41,565
at length return home; and she entered the house with the wish of appearing cheerful as usual,

393
00:37:41,565 --> 00:37:46,000
and the resolution of repressing such reflections as must make her unfit for conversation.

394
00:37:46,700 --> 00:37:51,400
She was immediately told, that the two gentlemen from Rosings had each called during her absence;

395
00:37:51,400 --> 00:37:56,065
Mr. Darcy, only for a few minutes, to take leave, but that Colonel Fitzwilliam had been

396
00:37:56,065 --> 00:38:00,565
sitting with them at least an hour, hoping for her return, and almost resolving to walk after

397
00:38:00,565 --> 00:38:11,433
her till she could be found. Elizabeth could but just affect concern in missing him; she really rejoiced at it. Colonel Fitzwilliam was no longer an object. She could think only of her letter.

398
00:38:11,433 --> 00:38:13,666
“His parting obeisance.”

399
00:38:13,666 --> 00:38:14,799
CHAPTER XXXVII.
